-- title:
--   Bayer–Stillman inductive step for colon ideals in degrees ≥ m
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field, let $n, m, g$ be natural numbers, and work in the polynomial ring $S = K[x_0,\dots,x_n]$ in $n+1$ variables, written as `MvPolynomial (Fin (n + 1)) K`. Let $F : \mathrm{Fin}\, g \to S$ be a family with each $F_i$ homogeneous of degree $m$, let $I = (F_1,\dots,F_g)$ be the ideal generated by the range of $F$, and let $\ell \in S$ be homogeneous of degree $1$. Two hypotheses are imposed: first, the colon condition in the single degree $m$, namely that every homogeneous $f$ of degree $m$ with $\ell f \in I$ already lies in $I$; second, that relations modulo $\ell$ are generated in degrees at most $1$, namely that for every natural number $d$ and every tuple $c : \mathrm{Fin}\, g \to S$ whose entries are all homogeneous of degree $d$ and which satisfies $\sum_i c_i F_i \in (\ell)$, the tuple $c$ lies in the $S$-submodule of $S^g$ spanned by those tuples $l$ for which there is some $d' \le 1$ with all entries $l_i$ homogeneous of degree $d'$ and $\sum_i l_i F_i \in (\ell)$. The conclusion is that for every $k \ge m$ and every $f \in S$ homogeneous of degree $k$ with $\ell f \in I$, one has $f \in I$; that is, $(I : \ell)_k = I_k$ for all $k \ge m$.
--
--   This is the inductive step in the Bayer–Stillman criterion for $m$-regularity: the colon condition in the single degree $m$, together with linear presentation of the image of $I$ in $S/(\ell)$, propagates the colon condition to all degrees $k \ge m$. It is used in the project to pass from a hypothesis on one graded piece to all higher pieces, in [`MvPolynomial.exists_forall_eval_ne_zero_mem_of_mul_mem_of_finrank_piece_succ_eq_macaulayPow`](thm.html#MvPolynomial.exists_forall_eval_ne_zero_mem_of_mul_mem_of_finrank_piece_succ_eq_macaulayPow).
